        "content": "# Requirements Specification: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-03-17\n\n## 1. Feature Overview\n\nThe project needs a CLI command that validates YAML files for correctness, reporting syntax errors and structural issues. This command must be implemented without introducing any new external dependencies, relying solely on Bun built-ins or existing project dependencies.\n\n## 2. Business Context\n\n### 2.1 Problem Statement\n\nUsers working with YAML configuration files currently have no built-in way to validate those files for syntax correctness within this project's toolchain. They must rely on external tools or discover errors only at runtime when the YAML is consumed by another process.\n\n### 2.2 Business Value\n\n- Reduces time spent debugging malformed YAML configuration files.\n- Provides a quick feedback loop for users authoring or editing YAML.\n- Keeps the project lightweight by avoiding new dependency overhead.\n\n### 2.3 Success Metrics\n\n- Users can validate any YAML file from the command line and receive a clear pass/fail result.\n- Zero new dependencies are added to `package.json`.\n- The command runs to completion within a reasonable time for typical YAML files (under a few hundred kilobytes).\n\n## 3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n### 3.1 User Types\n\n| User Type | Description | Relevance |\n|-----------|-------------|-----------|\n| Developer | A developer using this project's CLI tools to work with YAML files | Primary user; invokes the validate command directly |\n| CI Pipeline Operator | An automated system running validation as part of a build or deploy pipeline | Uses the command non-interactively, relies on exit codes |\n\n### 3.2 Stakeholder Interests\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest |\n|-------------|----------|\n| Project Maintainers | Keep the dependency footprint at zero external runtime deps; maintain code simplicity |\n| Developers | Get fast, accurate YAML validation feedback without installing separate tools |\n\n## 4. Scope Definition\n\n### 4.1 In Scope\n\n- A CLI command that accepts one or more file paths as arguments and validates each as YAML.\n- Clear, human-readable output indicating whether each file is valid or invalid.\n- For invalid files, a meaningful error message identifying the nature and location of the problem.\n- A non-zero exit code when any file fails validation (to support CI/scripting use cases).\n- An exit code of zero when all files pass validation.\n\n### 4.2 Out of Scope\n\n- YAML schema validation (validating against a specific schema or structure beyond syntax).\n- YAML linting or style enforcement (indentation preferences, key ordering, etc.).\n- Automatic YAML fixing or reformatting.\n- Reading YAML from stdin (may be added later but is not part of this initial scope).\n- Support for non-YAML formats (JSON, TOML, etc.).\n- Any new external dependency additions to the project.\n\n### 4.3 Assumptions\n\n- Bun's runtime or its standard library provides sufficient capability to parse YAML without external packages.\n- The command will follow the existing project patterns: TypeScript strict mode, ESM modules, kebab-case file naming, camelCase functions.\n- The project's existing test infrastructure (Bun test runner) is sufficient for testing this feature.\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### FR-001: Validate YAML File Syntax\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer, CI Pipeline Operator\n- **Requirement**: The command must accept one or more file paths as arguments and determine whether each file contains syntactically valid YAML.\n- **Rationale**: This is the core purpose of the feature -- providing a quick syntax check for YAML files.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: Given a valid YAML file, the command reports it as valid.\n  - AC-2: Given an invalid YAML file, the command reports it as invalid with a descriptive error message.\n  - AC-3: Given multiple file paths, the command validates each file independently and reports results for all of them.\n\n### FR-002: Provide Meaningful Error Messages\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: When a YAML file is invalid, the command must output an error message that helps the user locate and understand the problem, including at minimum the file name and the nature of the syntax error.\n- **Rationale**: A bare \"invalid\" message is not actionable. Users need enough detail to fix the problem.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: Error output includes the file path that failed.\n  - AC-2: Error output includes a description of the syntax error (e.g., \"unexpected indentation\", \"duplicate key\", or the parser's native error message).\n  - AC-3: If the parser provides a line number, it is included in the output.\n\n### FR-003: Return Appropriate Exit Codes\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: CI Pipeline Operator\n- **Requirement**: The command must exit with code 0 when all provided files are valid, and exit with a non-zero code when one or more files are invalid or cannot be read.\n- **Rationale**: CI pipelines and shell scripts rely on exit codes to determine success or failure.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: Exit code is 0 when all files are valid YAML.\n  - AC-2: Exit code is non-zero when any file is invalid YAML.\n  - AC-3: Exit code is non-zero when any specified file does not exist or cannot be read.\n\n### FR-004: Handle Missing or Unreadable Files\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer, CI Pipeline Operator\n- **Requirement**: If a specified file path does not exist or cannot be read, the command must report a clear error for that file rather than crashing.\n- **Rationale**: Graceful error handling prevents confusing stack traces and ensures all files in a batch are still processed.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: A non-existent file path produces a clear \"file not found\" error message.\n  - AC-2: The command continues processing remaining files after encountering a missing file.\n  - AC-3: The overall exit code reflects the failure.\n\n### FR-005: No New Dependencies\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Project Maintainers\n- **Requirement**: The feature must be implemented using only Bun built-ins or dependencies already present in the project. No new entries may be added to `package.json` dependencies or devDependencies for this feature.\n- **Rationale**: The project's design philosophy is zero external runtime dependencies. Adding dependencies increases maintenance burden and supply-chain risk.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: The `package.json` `dependencies` field (if it exists) has no new entries after this feature is implemented.\n  - AC-2: The `package.json` `devDependencies` field has no new entries related to YAML parsing after this feature is implemented.\n\n### FR-006: Display Usage Information\n- **Priority**: Should Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: When the command is invoked with no arguments or with a help flag, it should display brief usage instructions.\n- **Rationale**: Discoverability and self-documentation reduce the learning curve for new users.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: Running the command with no file arguments displays a usage message describing expected input.\n  - AC-2: The usage message includes at least one example invocation.\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### 6.1 Performance Expectations\n\n- The command should complete validation of a typical YAML file (under 100 KB) in under 1 second on standard developer hardware.\n- The command should handle files up to a few megabytes without crashing.\n\n### 6.2 Security Requirements\n\n- The command must only read files specified by the user; it must not write to or modify any file.\n- The command must not execute or evaluate any content within the YAML files.\n\n### 6.3 Usability Requirements\n\n- Output must be human-readable in a terminal.\n- Valid files should produce a brief confirmation message (not verbose output).\n- Error messages should be clearly distinguishable from success messages.\n\n### 6.4 Compliance Requirements\n\n- Must follow existing project code style: TypeScript strict mode, ESM modules, kebab-case filenames, camelCase functions.\n- Must include co-located tests following the existing `*.test.ts` pattern.\n\n## 7. User Stories\n\n### STORY-001: Validate a Single YAML File\n- **As a** developer\n- **I want to** run a CLI command with a YAML file path\n- **So that** I can quickly confirm the file is syntactically correct before using it\n\n**Acceptance (BDD)**:\n- GIVEN a syntactically valid YAML file at `config.yaml`\n- WHEN the user runs the validate command with `config.yaml` as an argument\n- THEN the command outputs a success message for that file and exits with code 0\n\n### STORY-002: Identify Errors in an Invalid YAML File\n- **As a** developer\n- **I want to** see a clear error message when my YAML file has a syntax problem\n- **So that** I can locate and fix the issue without guessing\n\n**Acceptance (BDD)**:\n- GIVEN a YAML file with a syntax error (e.g., bad indentation) at `broken.yaml`\n- WHEN the user runs the validate command with `broken.yaml` as an argument\n- THEN the command outputs an error message identifying the file and the nature of the error, and exits with a non-zero code\n\n### STORY-003: Validate Multiple Files in One Invocation\n- **As a** CI pipeline operator\n- **I want to** validate multiple YAML files in a single command invocation\n- **So that** I can check all configuration files in one pipeline step\n\n**Acceptance (BDD)**:\n- GIVEN three YAML files where two are valid and one is invalid\n- WHEN the user runs the validate command with all three file paths\n- THEN the command reports the status of each file individually and exits with a non-zero code\n\n### STORY-004: Handle Non-Existent File Gracefully\n- **As a** developer\n- **I want to** receive a clear error when I mistype a file path\n- **So that** I understand the problem is a missing file, not a YAML error\n\n**Acceptance (BDD)**:\n- GIVEN a file path `nonexistent.yaml` that does not exist\n- WHEN the user runs the validate command with that path\n- THEN the command outputs a \"file not found\" error and exits with a non-zero code\n\n### STORY-005: View Usage Information\n- **As a** developer new to the project\n- **I want to** see usage instructions when I run the command without arguments\n- **So that** I know how to use it correctly\n\n**Acceptance (BDD)**:\n- GIVEN the user runs the validate command with no arguments\n- WHEN the command executes\n- THEN a usage message with syntax and an example is displayed\n\n## 8. Business Rules\n\n- BR-001: The YAML validator must treat any file that cannot be parsed as valid YAML 1.1 or 1.2 as invalid.\n- BR-002: Empty files should be treated as valid YAML (an empty YAML document is valid per the YAML specification).\n- BR-003: Files with multiple YAML documents (separated by `---`) should be validated in their entirety; all documents within the file must be valid for the file to be considered valid.\n- BR-004: The command is read-only; it must never modify any file on disk.\n\n## 9. Dependencies & Constraints\n\n| Dependency/Constraint | Description |\n|-----------------------|-------------|\n| Bun Runtime | The command runs in the Bun runtime environment; YAML parsing must use capabilities available in Bun's standard library or built-in APIs |\n| No New Packages | No new npm packages may be introduced for this feature |\n| Existing Project Structure | The command must integrate with the existing `src/` directory layout and `package.json` scripts |\n| TypeScript Strict Mode | All new code must compile under TypeScript strict mode |\n\n## 10. Clarifications Log\n\n| # | Question | Resolution | Source |\n|---|----------|------------|--------|\n| 1 | Which YAML specification version should be supported? | Support whatever version Bun's built-in YAML capabilities handle (conservatively YAML 1.1/1.2) | Inferred from no-new-deps constraint |\n| 2 | Should stdin input be supported? | No, file path arguments only for initial scope | Conservative default; out-of-scope decision |\n| 3 | Should glob patterns be supported for file paths? | No, explicit file paths only for initial scope | Conservative default |\n| 4 | What constitutes \"no new dependencies\" -- runtime only or devDependencies too? | No new dependencies of any kind related to YAML parsing; existing devDependencies like @types/bun are acceptable | Inferred from project philosophy (patterns.md: \"No external dependencies beyond Bun built-ins\") |\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Defaults\n\n| Decision Point | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------------|--------|-----------|\n| PRD selection | None (no PRDs found) | No PRDs directory or files exist in the project |\n| Charter association | None (no charter found) | No charter.md file exists at .rp1/context/charter.md |\n\n## AFK Mode: Inferred Decisions\n\n| Ambiguity | Resolution | Source |\n|-----------|------------|--------|\n| \"simple\" validator -- what level of validation? | Syntax validation only, no schema validation | Conservative interpretation of \"simple\"; schema validation would add significant complexity |\n| \"without adding new deps\" -- scope of restriction | No new entries in package.json whatsoever for YAML parsing | patterns.md states \"No external dependencies beyond Bun built-ins\" |\n| Which users will use this command | Developers and CI pipeline operators | Inferred from CLI tool nature and project context (utility scripting domain) |\n| How should the command be invoked | As a Bun script (e.g., `bun run src/yaml-validator.ts`) or as a registered package.json script | Follows existing pattern where `bun run src/index.ts` is the current entry point model |\n| Error output format | Human-readable text to stderr/stdout, not JSON | Consistent with project's simple, minimal approach; no structured output requirement stated |\n| Empty file handling | Treat as valid YAML | Per YAML specification, an empty document is valid |\n| Multi-document YAML files | Validate all documents within the file | Conservative: validate everything rather than only the first document |\n"
      },
      "source": "assistant"
