        "content": "# Design: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Complete\n**Created**: 2026-03-17\n\n---\n\n## 1. Design Overview\n\nA CLI command that validates YAML file syntax, built as a simple script-based utility following the existing project architecture. The command accepts file paths as arguments, validates each for YAML syntax correctness, and reports human-readable results with appropriate exit codes.\n\nThe design introduces two new source files (an entry point and a logic module) plus a co-located test file, following the same separation-of-concerns pattern already established by `src/index.ts` and `src/utils.ts`.\n\n### High-Level Architecture\n\n```mermaid\ngraph LR\n    A[CLI Invocation<br/>bun run validate-yaml] --> B[validate-yaml.ts<br/>Entry Point]\n    B --> C[yaml-validator.ts<br/>Validation Logic]\n    C --> D[Bun.file API<br/>File Reading]\n    C --> E[YAML Parser<br/>Bun Built-in]\n    B --> F[Console Output<br/>stdout / stderr]\n    B --> G[Process Exit Code<br/>0 or 1]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Architecture\n\n### Component Architecture\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TB\n    subgraph CLI[\"CLI Layer (validate-yaml.ts)\"]\n        A1[parseArgs: Parse process.argv]\n        A2[showUsage: Display help text]\n        A3[main: Orchestrate validation loop]\n        A4[formatResult: Format output strings]\n    end\n\n    subgraph Core[\"Validation Layer (yaml-validator.ts)\"]\n        B1[validateYamlFile: Read + validate a file path]\n        B2[validateYamlContent: Parse YAML string]\n        B3[readFileContent: Safe file reading]\n    end\n\n    A3 --> B1\n    B1 --> B3\n    B1 --> B2\n```\n\n### Request Flow (Sequence)\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant User\n    participant CLI as validate-yaml.ts\n    participant Val as yaml-validator.ts\n    participant FS as Bun.file()\n    participant YAML as YAML Parser\n\n    User->>CLI: bun run validate-yaml file1.yaml file2.yaml\n    CLI->>CLI: parseArgs(process.argv)\n\n    loop For each file path\n        CLI->>Val: validateYamlFile(filePath)\n        Val->>FS: Bun.file(filePath).text()\n        alt File not found / unreadable\n            FS-->>Val: Error\n            Val-->>CLI: { valid: false, error: \"File not found...\" }\n        else File read successfully\n            FS-->>Val: fileContent\n            alt Empty file\n                Val-->>CLI: { valid: true }\n            else Non-empty content\n                Val->>YAML: parse(content)\n                alt Parse succeeds\n                    YAML-->>Val: parsed result\n                    Val-->>CLI: { valid: true }\n                else Parse fails\n                    YAML-->>Val: SyntaxError with details\n                    Val-->>CLI: { valid: false, error: details }\n                end\n            end\n        end\n        CLI->>User: Print result for file\n    end\n\n    CLI->>CLI: process.exit(allValid ? 0 : 1)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Detailed Design\n\n### 3.1 Data Types\n\n```typescript\n/** Result of validating a single YAML file */\ninterface ValidationResult {\n  filePath: string;\n  valid: boolean;\n  error?: string;\n  line?: number;\n  column?: number;\n}\n```\n\nNo persistent data storage is involved. All data flows through function parameters and return values within a single process invocation.\n\n### 3.2 Module: `src/yaml-validator.ts` (Validation Logic)\n\nPure functions with no side effects. This module handles file I/O and YAML parsing, returning structured results.\n\n**Exported Functions**:\n\n| Function | Signature | Purpose |\n|----------|-----------|---------|\n| `validateYamlFile` | `(filePath: string) => Promise<ValidationResult>` | Reads a file and validates its YAML content |\n| `validateYamlContent` | `(content: string, filePath: string) => ValidationResult` | Validates a YAML string (handles multi-doc) |\n\n**Internal Logic for `validateYamlFile`**:\n1. Attempt to read the file using `Bun.file(filePath).text()`\n2. If file read fails (not found, permission error), return `{ valid: false, error: \"File not found or unreadable: <filePath>\" }`\n3. If file content is empty (zero-length or whitespace-only), return `{ valid: true }` (per BR-002)\n4. Delegate to `validateYamlContent`\n\n**Internal Logic for `validateYamlContent`**:\n1. Split content on `---` document separators to handle multi-document YAML (BR-003)\n2. For each document segment, attempt YAML parsing\n3. If any segment fails, return the first error encountered with location info\n4. If all segments parse successfully, return `{ valid: true }`\n\n**YAML Parsing Strategy**:\nThe parsing mechanism depends on what Bun provides as a built-in. The design isolates parsing behind `validateYamlContent` so the parsing implementation can be swapped without affecting the CLI layer or test interfaces.\n\n### 3.3 Module: `src/validate-yaml.ts` (CLI Entry Point)\n\nHandles argument parsing, orchestration, output formatting, and process exit.\n\n**Functions**:\n\n| Function | Signature | Purpose |\n|----------|-----------|---------|\n| `parseArgs` | `(argv: string[]) => { files: string[]; help: boolean }` | Extracts file paths and flags from argv |\n| `showUsage` | `() => void` | Prints usage information to stdout |\n| `formatResult` | `(result: ValidationResult) => string` | Formats a result for terminal display |\n| `main` | `() => Promise<void>` | Orchestrates the full command flow |\n\n**CLI Argument Parsing**:\n- `process.argv.slice(2)` to get user-provided arguments (Bun convention, same as `src/index.ts`)\n- If `--help` or `-h` is present, show usage and exit 0\n- If no file arguments, show usage and exit 1\n- All other arguments are treated as file paths\n\n**Output Format**:\n```\n# Success:\nPASS  path/to/valid-file.yaml\n\n# Failure (with line info):\nFAIL  path/to/invalid-file.yaml\n  Error: unexpected indentation at line 12, column 5\n\n# Failure (file not found):\nFAIL  path/to/missing-file.yaml\n  Error: File not found or unreadable\n\n# Summary (when multiple files):\nResults: 2 passed, 1 failed\n```\n\n**Exit Code Logic**:\n- Exit 0: All files validated successfully\n- Exit 1: One or more files failed validation or could not be read\n\n---\n\n## 4. Technology Stack\n\n| Category | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------|--------|-----------|\n| Runtime | Bun | Existing project runtime |\n| Language | TypeScript (strict mode) | Existing project language |\n| Module System | ESM | Existing project convention |\n| File I/O | `Bun.file()` API | Bun built-in, async, efficient |\n| YAML Parsing | Bun built-in YAML support | Zero-dependency constraint (see HYP-001) |\n| Testing | `bun:test` | Existing test infrastructure |\n| Process Args | `process.argv` | Standard, matches existing entry point pattern |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Implementation Plan\n\n| # | Component | Description | Files |\n|---|-----------|-------------|-------|\n| T1 | YAML Validation Logic | Core validation functions: file reading, YAML parsing, result structuring | `src/yaml-validator.ts` |\n| T2 | CLI Entry Point | Argument parsing, orchestration, output formatting, exit codes | `src/validate-yaml.ts` |\n| T3 | Unit Tests | Tests for validation logic covering valid, invalid, empty, multi-doc, missing files | `src/yaml-validator.test.ts` |\n| T4 | Script Registration | Add `validate-yaml` script to `package.json` | `package.json` |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Implementation DAG\n\n**Parallel Groups** (tasks with no inter-dependencies):\n\n1. [T1] - Foundation module with no internal dependencies\n2. [T2, T3, T4] - All depend on T1 interfaces but are independent of each other\n\n**Dependencies**:\n\n- T2 -> T1 (Interface: CLI imports `validateYamlFile` from yaml-validator)\n- T3 -> T1 (Interface: Tests import `validateYamlFile`, `validateYamlContent` from yaml-validator)\n- T4 -> T2 (Sequential: Script entry references `src/validate-yaml.ts` which must exist)\n\n**Critical Path**: T1 -> T2 -> T4\n\n---\n\n## 7. Testing Strategy\n\n### Test Value Assessment\n\n| Test Category | Value | Rationale |\n|---------------|-------|-----------|\n| Valid YAML parsing | HIGH | Core business logic of the feature |\n| Invalid YAML error reporting | HIGH | Core business logic; error message quality is a key requirement |\n| Multi-document YAML handling | HIGH | Business rule BR-003; app-specific behavior |\n| Empty file handling | MEDIUM | Business rule BR-002; edge case with defined behavior |\n| Missing file handling | HIGH | FR-004; app-specific error handling |\n| CLI argument parsing | MEDIUM | App-specific logic for arg extraction |\n| Exit code correctness | HIGH | FR-003; critical for CI integration |\n\n### Tests NOT Designed (low value)\n\n| Avoided Test | Reason |\n|--------------|--------|\n| YAML parser correctly parses valid YAML | Tests the parser library, not our code |\n| Bun.file() reads files correctly | Tests Bun runtime behavior |\n| process.exit() sets exit code | Tests Node/Bun runtime behavior |\n\n### Test Plan: `src/yaml-validator.test.ts`\n\n```typescript\ndescribe(\"yaml-validator\", () => {\n  describe(\"validateYamlContent\", () => {\n    test(\"returns valid for correct YAML\");\n    test(\"returns valid for empty content\");\n    test(\"returns invalid with error details for malformed YAML\");\n    test(\"returns line number in error when available\");\n    test(\"validates all documents in multi-document YAML\");\n    test(\"returns invalid if any document in multi-doc YAML is malformed\");\n  });\n\n  describe(\"validateYamlFile\", () => {\n    test(\"returns valid for a valid YAML file\");\n    test(\"returns invalid for an invalid YAML file\");\n    test(\"returns error for non-existent file path\");\n    test(\"returns valid for an empty file\");\n  });\n});\n```\n\nTests use temporary files created in test setup (`beforeEach`/`afterEach`) for file-based tests, or test `validateYamlContent` directly with string inputs for pure logic tests.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Deployment Design\n\nNo deployment infrastructure changes required. The feature is a local CLI tool.\n\n**Integration**:\n- Add script to `package.json`: `\"validate-yaml\": \"bun run src/validate-yaml.ts\"`\n- Invocation: `bun run validate-yaml file1.yaml file2.yaml`\n- Direct invocation: `bun src/validate-yaml.ts file1.yaml file2.yaml`\n\n---\n\n## 9. Documentation Impact\n\n| Type | Target | Section | KB Source | Rationale |\n|------|--------|---------|-----------|-----------|\n| edit | `.rp1/context/modules.md` | Modules | modules.md | Add entries for new validate-yaml and yaml-validator modules |\n| edit | `.rp1/context/architecture.md` | Scripts | architecture.md | Add validate-yaml script to the Scripts section |\n| edit | `.rp1/context/index.md` | Project Structure | index.md | Add new files to the src/ directory listing |\n\n---\n\n## 10. Design Decisions Log\n\nSee `design-decisions.md` for the full decisions log.\n\nKey decisions:\n- **D1**: Separate entry point from validation logic (follows existing index.ts/utils.ts pattern)\n- **D2**: Use `Bun.file()` API for file reading (Bun-native, async, built-in)\n- **D3**: Split multi-document YAML on `---` for individual validation (satisfies BR-003)\n- **D4**: PASS/FAIL output format with indented error details (clear terminal readability)\n- **D5**: Isolate YAML parsing behind `validateYamlContent` abstraction (mitigates parser availability risk)\n"
